    historyContent: {
      origins: "Part of the larger North European 'Nordic Casino' group, it was brought to Sweden in the 19th century. It diverged from traditional English and Russian Casino through the inclusion of 'Royal' face card values (J=11, Q=12, K=13).",
      originCountries: [
        'Sweden',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1880s: First documented in Swedish gaming manuals.',
        "1920s: The 'Storan/Lillan' terminology becomes universal in Swedish households.",
        "Present: Popular family game, known for being easy to learn but difficult to master due to the 16-point 'In-Hand' rule.",
      ],
      evolution: 'The Swedish variant retained a slightly more balanced scoring system than the Finnish version, with Spades being less dominant over other categories.',
      cultural: 'In Sweden, clearing the table is called a tabbe (or tabellå), worth one extra point, similar to Finnish mökki.',
